+   <details class="vdb-yol"><summary>Show the diagnostic path</summary><ol class="vdb-akis"><li><span class="vdb-ak-e"><b>Symptom</b></span><p>Intermittent converter overcurrent fault. Normal after a reset; every measurement on the stopped turbine is normal.</p></li><li><span class="vdb-ak-e"><b>Measurement</b></span><p>Fault log matched with power data. A thermal scan under load showed a DC busbar joint ~30 °C hotter than its neighbours; bolt torque below the OEM value.</p></li><li><span class="vdb-ak-e"><b>Root cause</b></span><p>A loosened DC busbar joint. It heats up at high current and also stresses the neighbouring IGBT module.</p></li><li><span class="vdb-ak-e"><b>Solution</b></span><p>Joint cleaned and tightened to OEM torque, every joint in the cabinet checked, the stressed IGBT replaced. Production continued meanwhile with a 2.2 MW limit.</p></li><li><span class="vdb-ak-e"><b>Lesson</b></span><p>With an intermittent fault, look for the condition it appears in, not the fault itself. Do thermal scans while the turbine is producing.</p></li></ol></details>
